    "q-114146": {
      "dom_id": "step-q114146",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Solve ${n}^{3}+4{n}^{2}-9n-36=0.$",
      "worked_solution": "We proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{3}+4{n}^{2}-9n-36 & = \\\\ ({n}^{3}+4{n}^{2})-(9n+36) & = \\\\ {n}^{2}(n+4)-9(n+4) & = \\\\ (n+4)\\cdot {n}^{2}-(n+4)\\cdot 9 & = \\\\ (n+4)({n}^{2}-9) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(n+4)({n}^{2}-9)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(n+4)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n+4 & =0 \\\\ n & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $({n}^{2}-9)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-9 & =0 \\\\ {n}^{2} & =9 \\\\ n & =\\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $n=-4,\\,\\pm 3.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-114146-a",
