    "q-114145": {
      "dom_id": "step-q114145",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Solve ${m}^{3}-3{m}^{2}-2m+6=0.$",
      "worked_solution": "We proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{3}-3{m}^{2}-2m+6 & = \\\\ ({m}^{3}-3{m}^{2})-(2m-6) & = \\\\ {m}^{2}(m-3)-2(m-3) & = \\\\ (m-3)\\cdot {m}^{2}-(m-3)\\cdot 2 & = \\\\ (m-3)({m}^{2}-2) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(m-3)({m}^{2}-2)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(m-3)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m-3 & =0 \\\\ m & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $({m}^{2}-2)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-2 & =0 \\\\ {m}^{2} & =2 \\\\ m & =\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $m=3,\\,\\pm \\sqrt{2}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
